\begin_layout Section*
Introduction and aims
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sooner or later, the pavement structures will reach the end of their useful
 life and will need to be rehabilitated.
 Flexible road structures can be reinforced by the addition of a new layer
 of asphalt, where the required thickness of the strengthening layer is
 usually determined by the elastic deformation of the existing deteriorated
 pavement structure under standard loading.
 However, a problem in correctly interpreting the observed deformations
 is to eliminate stiffness variations due to the viscoelastic behavior of
 asphalt materials – as a function of temperature and loading frequency
 – and the varying moisture content of the subgrade, which is also a basic
 requirement for accurate pavement structure diagnostics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Many elements of existing and new pavement design procedures now follow
 the design principles of mechanics, so it is possible to take these effects
 into account or correct for them.
 The primary objective of this research is therefore to develop a mechanically
 based pavement structure design framework that uses in-situ pavement structure
 diagnostics in addition to laboratory material testing for structural analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The background of the research is the work of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "toth2020newhungarian"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 entitled 
\emph on

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

New Hungarian Mechanistic-Empirical Design Procedure for Asphalt Pavements
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, where the authors present an alternative design procedure for the design
 and construction of new asphalt pavement structures, which allows to better
 take into account the potential of the sub-grade and the material properties
 of the constructed pavement layers compared to the application of standard
 pavement structures.
 The alternative design procedure, however, only models the behavior of
 asphalt layers as an elastic material and should be further developed towards
 viscoelastic calculations.
 To this end, the thesis focuses on the following sub-tasks:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Analysis of the interaction between wheel load and road structure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Development of a new material model for asphalt mixtures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Develop a method to determine the load frequency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first point is important because it has a major influence on the stresses
 induced in the flexible road structure under evaluation.
 It is important to know that the contact pressure 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 depends on the wheel load and the type of selected tire.
 The aim of this work is to propose a simple computational method to account
 for the contact behavior of the complex finite element tire model in programs
 using the much simpler elastic layer theory.
 The impact of model simplification can be measured by the magnitude of
 the stresses experienced in road structures, and this requires comparative
 calculations between layered elastic analysis computer programs and finite
 element tire models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second point concerns the so-called asphalt mix master curve.
 This is based on the time-temperature superposition principle known from
 rheology.
 From a practical point of view, it is not easy to incorporate the dynamic
 modulus determined from laboratory data at different temperatures and frequenci
es into mechanical calculations.
 This is because, in reality, road structures are not loaded in the frequency
 domain but in the time domain.
 The third point is related to this issue and is the relationship between
 the moving wheel load and the load frequency.
 The load frequency is a critical parameter for laboratory testing of asphalt
 mixes and must therefore be able to be matched to the wheel speed of the
 moving vehicles in the mechanistic-empirical (M-E) design system.
\end_layout

