\begin_layout Section*
New RAMBO material model for asphalt mixes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sigmoid functions can be widely used for the description of the viscoelastic
 properties of asphalt mixes.
 Unfortunately, closed relationships between known model coefficients in
 time or frequency domain are not known.
 Therefore, to convert viscoelastic material properties into each other,
 complicated interconversion methods have to be used in many cases.
 To solve this problem, a new RAMBO material model is presented in this
 thesis.
 It describes the temperature and frequency dependent behavior of asphalt
 mixtures based on Kramers-Kronig relations (fig.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:rambo-graphical-abstract"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The new model is based on the mathematical form of the Ramberg-Osgood material
 model and its main advantage is that its coefficients are completely independen
t of each other.
 The RAMBO model is a good alternative to the sigmoid function for describing
 stiffness data, and the independence of its coefficients allows the full
 morphology of the master curve to be characterized numerically.
\end_layout

