 The analysis of the data has shown that the time-frequency conversion factors
 are only dependent on the slope of the new empirical model.
 This means that no additional computations are required.
 The RAMBO models determined in the frequency domain provide an excellent
 characterization of the mixture in the time domain.
 The developed new empirical material model has been verified with laboratory
 data and accurate numerical calculations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper shows that the conversion of the viscoelastic material properties
 of asphalt mixes from the frequency domain to the time domain depends not
 only on the reciprocity between the two variables but also on the parameter
 
\emph on
R
\emph default
 of the RAMBO material model, which determines the slope of the master curve
 between the upper and lower asymptotic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Method for determining the load frequency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mechanistic-empirical pavement design methods require knowing the dynamic
 modulus master curve of asphalt mixes to design.
 This requires the engineer to correctly determine the loading frequency
 along with the temperature of the paved structure.
 Frequency is also a key parameter for laboratory fatigue testing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the real world, accurate frequency estimation for design is a difficult
 task because pavement structures are not loaded in the frequency domain
 but in the time domain.
 To solve this problem, researchers have developed various time-frequency
 conversion methods to accurately calculate the corresponding frequency
 from the duration of traffic-generated stress or strain pulses.
 However, the latest results show that the time-frequency equivalence factor
 required for the conversion is much lower than the one used by researchers
 in the past.
\end_layout

