\begin_layout Section*
Theses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
The mechanistic-empirical (M-E) pavement design systems commonly use an
 approximation that assumes the tire's internal inflation pressure is nearly
 equal to the specific contact pressure the tire transfers to the pavement.
 However, this overestimates values typical of diagonal tires.
 The average contact pressure of a tire depends not only on the internal
 inflation pressure but also on the tire's flexural constant.
 The pressure model used to establish the relationship is limited to the
 depth at which the measured vertical compressive stress in the road structure
 is less than the average contact pressure value.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\size small
The contact pressure values calculated by the finite element method from
 the diagonal tire model built from literature data were compared with the
 laboratory results of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "vanvuuren1974relationship"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and good agreement was found.
 This is because stress peaks caused by non-uniform load distribution, which
 are typical of overloaded tires, are equalized 5–8 centimeters below the
 pavement surface.
 The simple pressure model proposed by can accurately account for wheel
 loads in pavement structure design.
 The proposed wheel pressure model is suitable for mechanistic-empirical
 (M-E) pavement design
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q=0.454p+197.87
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is the average contact pressure and 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is the internal tire pressure in kPa.
\end_layout

