\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
The Ramberg-Osgood relationship's mathematical form is suitable for describing
 the temperature and frequency-dependent behavior of asphalt mixtures.
 The coefficients of the new four-parameter RAMBO model are independent
 of each other and remain constant when moving from the frequency domain
 to the time domain.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\size small
The new model is based on the paper by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "kweon2008application"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, which successfully applied the mathematical form of the Ramberg-Osgood
 material model to describe the dynamic modulus 
\begin_inset Formula $E^{\ast}$
\end_inset

 master curve of asphalt mixtures:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E^{\ast}\left(f_{r}\right)=E_{e}^{*}+\frac{E_{g}^{*}-E_{e}^{*}}{1+Cf_{r}^{R-1}}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{e}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is the asymptotic of low frequencies or high temperatures, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{g}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is the asymptotic of high frequencies or low temperatures, 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{r}$
\end_inset

 is the reduced frequency, 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 are the material-dependent constants.
 The thesis compares the dynamic modulus master curves obtained with the
 RAMBO and sigmoid function and establishes a relationship between their
 model coefficients.
 It is shown that the RAMBO model is a good substitute for the sigmoid function
 in describing stiffness data, and its coefficients are independent, allowing
 for the numerical characterization of the full morphology of the master
 curve.
 The RAMBO model was extended to predict the phase angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 using 
\size default
\lang magyar

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citeauthor
key "kramers1927ladiffusion"
literal "false"

\end_inset

-
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citeauthor
key "kronig1926onthe"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\size small
\lang english
 relations:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi\left(f_{r}\right)=-c\frac{\pi}{2}\dfrac{\left(E_{g}^{*}-E_{e}^{*}\right)\left(R-1\right)Cf_{r}^{R-1}}{\left(Cf_{r}^{R-1}+1\right)\left(E_{e}^{*}Cf_{r}^{R-1}+E_{g}^{*}\right)}
\]

\end_inset

Comparative calculations have shown that the new phase angle function matches
 the results of the general Maxwell model very closely.
 Additionally, time-frequency conversion relations (
\begin_inset Formula $k_{r}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{c}$
\end_inset

) have been developed to generate time-domain master curves of material
 parameters with model parameters in the frequency domain:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{alignat*}{1}
E\left(t_{r}\right) & =E_{e}^{*}+\frac{E_{g}^{*}-E_{e}^{*}}{1+C\left(k_{r}t_{r}\right)^{1-R}}\\
\\
D\left(t_{r}\right) & =\frac{1+C\left(k_{c}t_{r}\right)^{1-R}}{E_{g}^{*}+E_{e}^{*}C\left(k_{c}t_{r}\right)^{1-R}}
\end{alignat*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(t_{r}\right)$
\end_inset

 is the relaxation modulus, 
\begin_inset Formula $D\left(t_{r}\right)$
\end_inset

 is the creep compliance and 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{r}$
\end_inset

 is the reduced time.
\end_layout

