\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\size small
To determine the constants 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{r}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{c}$
\end_inset

 for the time-frequency conversion, model calculations were performed.
 A database of 400 hypothetical master curves was created by varying the
 RAMBO model parameters over a wide range.
 The relaxation modulus and creep compliance of the hypothetical master
 curves were estimated using the relations of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ninomiya1959someapproximate"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, which proved to be a good choice.
 Finally, regression calculations were used to determine the relationship
 between the coefficients of the RAMBO model and the constants 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{r}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{c}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
k_{r}= & 16.31-7.04R\\
k_{c}= & 5.150+6.26R
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where 
\emph on
R
\emph default
 is the shape parameter of the RAMBO material model.
 The proposed relation for time-frequency conversion:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\dfrac{1}{k_{r}t}=\frac{\beta}{t}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=1/k_{r}$
\end_inset

 time-frequency equivalence factor.
 Comparative calculations have shown that the RAMBO model coefficients defined
 in the frequency domain also provide an excellent characterization of the
 mixture under study in the time domain.
\end_layout

