]
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
The relationship between the speed of the moving wheel load (
\emph on
V
\emph default
) and the load frequency of the dynamic modulus (
\emph on
f
\emph default
) is determined by the time-frequency equivalence factor of the asphalt
 layer in the road structure and the wavelength of the mechanical response
 (stress or deformation).
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\size small
The model is based on the distance-time physics relation.
 The time-frequency equivalence factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 of the RAMBO material model was used for the time-frequency conversion:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\frac{\beta}{\lambda}V^{k}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is the wavelength of the mechanical response of the asphalt layer to stress
 or deformation, 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 is the speed of the moving wheel load, and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is an experimental constant whose value is assumed to be one in the general
 case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\size small
Related part of the thesis: 
\series bold
chapter 5
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\size small
Publications related to the thesis: [
\series bold
P8
\series default
]
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
From the deflection curve measured on the pavement surface, the deformation
 wavelength of the asphalt layer under test can be determined.
 The wavelength is equal to twice the distance marked by the inflection
 point on the curvature plot of the function fitted to the measured vertical
 deformations.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\size small
For the model, I used the 
\begin_inset Formula $D\left(r\right)$
\end_inset

 deflection function proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "primusz2009abehajlasi"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, from which I approximated the curvature change by 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa\left(r\right)\approx D^{''}\left(r\right)$
\end_inset

 (fig.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:func-def-curvature"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 Taking into account that, according to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jung1988directcalculation"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, the radius of curvature at the center of the load gives the approximate
 value of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{xx}$
\end_inset

 strain at the bottom of the asphalt layer of thickness 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

, the inflection point 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{\textrm{inf}}$
\end_inset

 of the function 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa\left(r\right)$
\end_inset

 can be taken as the strain wavelength of the specific elongation at the
 bottom of the asphalt layer of thickness 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\lambda=2r_{\textrm{inf}}=\frac{4a_{c}}{\sqrt{3c}}
\]

\end_inset

According to the derived relationship, the deformation wavelength 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 depends on the contact radius 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{c}$
\end_inset

 of the load wheel and the shape parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 of the deflection curve.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\size small
Related part of the thesis: 
\series bold
chapter 5
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\size small
Publications related to the thesis: [
\series bold
P8
\series default
]
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
For periodic loading of asphalt mixtures, the load frequency in the mechanistic-
empirical (M-E) design system should be set according to the mechanical
 response under test.
 In the M-E pavement structure design systems, a loading frequency of 10
 Hz is recommended for stress mechanical response, while a loading frequency
 of 5 Hz is recommended for strain mechanical response.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\size small
When considering a stress pulse, the wavelength is approximated by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=2a_{c}$
\end_inset

, so the load frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 can be calculated using the following general formula:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\frac{\beta}{2a_{c}}V^{k}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is the time-frequency equivalence factor, 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{c}$
\end_inset

 is the contact radius of the load wheel, 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 is the speed of the moving wheel, and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is an experimental constant.
 For the asphalt mixtures considered in this thesis, 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{r}=12$
\end_inset

 and therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=1/12=0.085$
\end_inset

 and the contact radius of the twin tires 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{c}=\unit[0.12]{m}$
\end_inset

, so substituting this into the stress pulse formula gives the simple relationsh
ip below:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f\approx0.1V
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 is used in km/h.
 The approximate relationship confirms the value of 10 Hz proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "witzcak2002simpleperformance"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, that at typical highway speeds (~110 km/h) the load pulse duration is
 approximately 0.1 s.
 When examining a strain pulse, the wavelength is approximated by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=4a_{c}/\sqrt{3c}$
\end_inset

, so that the load frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 can be calculated using the following general formula:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\sqrt{0.75c}\frac{\beta}{2a_{c}}V^{k}
\]

\end_inset

Taking into account that the deflection curve shape factor 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 varies between 0.2 and 0.4 for flexible and especially flexible pavement
 structures, the correction factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{0.75c}$
\end_inset

 limits the load frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 to a range between 3.8 Hz and 5.3 Hz with respect to the stress pulse.
\end_layout

